{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DxMpnywCu9eCqSTax2Jczg","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval needs, none requiring a rewrite of the paper story.\n\n## Overall assessment\nThe draft is densely cited on Bradley-Terry estimation (ties, order effects, existence of the MLE, penalised fits, asymptotics) and on sampling uncertainty. It also cites the single-point influence-function paper, the dropping-data robustness paper, the two Arena integrity papers, and the Arena and MT-Bench data sources. All quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are out of scope here. The gaps concern the paper's actual subject (small adversarial edits to comparison data), the caveats on first-order approximations, and the sports data.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Poisoning and manipulation literature (high, positioning) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** the \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n- **Why:** the paper asks how few dropped or reversed rows displace rank 1, which is a data-poisoning or manipulation question. No citation covers influence-based poisoning, label-flip attacks, or adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation and rating systems. The \"generic, evaluator-verified probe\" framing cannot be positioned without this, and reviewers would likely flag it.\n- **Search topics:** influence-function-based data poisoning; label-flipping attacks on logistic or pairwise models; adversarial robustness of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons; manipulation-resistant rating systems (Elo / BT).\n\n### 2. Accuracy of first-order group-removal approximations (medium, background) — Problem Setting and Method\n- **Anchor:** \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change…\". The \"Linearisation error\" hypothesis in the analysis section relies on the same claim.\n- **Why:** the claim that the additive first-order heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2% budget is uncited. Only single-point influence and the dropping-data robustness paper are cited.\n- **Search topics:** accuracy of influence functions for group effects; infinitesimal-jackknife approximation error when dropping data in M-estimators or logistic regression.\n\n### 3. Sports rating context (medium, benchmark_context) — Problem Setting and Method\n- **Anchor:** Table 1 row \"Sports: NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first\".\n- **Why:** NBA results are used as a fourth BT design (and called a \"sports proxy\" in Limitations) without any citation to BT or Elo-style rating of sports teams. One or two references would justify the design and the home-team-first and tie conventions. The Arena and MT-Bench rows are already cited.\n- **Search topics:** Bradley-Terry rating of sports teams with home advantage; Elo rating applied to basketball or team sports.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- BT model variants and estimation: already over-supported.\n- Uncertainty of rankings: adequately cited.\n- LLM-judge and Arena data descriptions: covered by the Chiang and Zheng citations.\n- Numerical results, reference-policy scores and validity checks: grounded in `paper_data.json`.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261028_212829.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261028_212829.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. Five queries, all driven by `draft_reference_gaps.{md,json}`; no retrieval performed in this stage.\n\n## Central topic\nSensitivity of Bradley-Terry leaderboard rankings (rank-1 displacement) to small, evaluator-verified drop/flip edits across crowd, expert-human, LLM-judge and NBA sports designs. This plan only fills the three gaps exposed by the draft.\n\n## Existing bibliography (not re-searched)\nBradley-Terry variants, ties/order effects, MLE existence, asymptotics and ridge fits; ranking uncertainty; Koh & Liang influence functions; Broderick et al. dropping-data robustness; Min et al. (vote rigging); Singh et al. (Leaderboard Illusion); Chiang et al. (Arena); Zheng et al. (MT-Bench).\n\n## Queries\n\n### 1. Poisoning and manipulation positioning (high, Related Work)\nAnchor: \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n1. `influence function data poisoning attack training set perturbation logistic regression` (baseline_comparator): influence-based poisoning that chooses edits, the closest relative of the drop/flip probe.\n2. `label flipping poisoning attack adversarial robustness pairwise comparison rank aggregation manipulation` (baseline_comparator): flip mode as a label-flip edit; adversarial robustness of rank aggregation.\n3. `manipulation-resistant rating systems Elo Bradley-Terry strategic manipulation of match outcomes` (baseline_comparator): general manipulation of rating systems beyond the two Arena papers.\n\n### 2. First-order group-removal accuracy (medium, Problem Setting and Method)\nAnchor: \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change...\" and the \"Linearisation error\" hypothesis.\n4. `accuracy of influence functions for group effects removing multiple training points infinitesimal jackknife approximation error` (core_method): supports the claim that additive first-order approximations can be inaccurate at a 2% budget. One query covers both group-influence and infinitesimal-jackknife error.\n\n### 3. Sports rating context (medium, Problem Setting and Method)\nAnchor: Table 1 row \"Sports: NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first\" and the \"sports proxy\" limitation.\n5. `Bradley-Terry model home advantage rating of basketball and sports teams Elo` (benchmark_context): justifies NBA results as BT data and contextualises the home-first and tie conventions.\n\n## Exclusions\n- No re-retrieval of BT variants, ties, MLE existence, asymptotics or ranking uncertainty (the gap memo marks them over-supported).\n- No re-retrieval of the Arena/MT-Bench data papers or already-cited influence, dropping-data and rigging papers.\n- No queries about papers that directly study dropping preferences to change LLM leaderboard top ranks (excluded by the task's no-access rule).\n- No claims about the re-linearised or prefix-search variants, since the run has no scored winner.\n5924 paper.tex\n- Framing: measurement/limitations paper on verified first-order influence rank-1 displacement across crowd, expert-human, LLM-judge and sports designs (final.metric.*: 0.606 family-averaged; crowd 1.000, sports 0.833, expert 0.374, LLM judge 0.216). Final outcome qualified/completed (final.qualification.*). Rejected: method-improvement (0/3 variants scored) and noise-vs-intent framing (no experiments).\n- Grounding decisions: protocol facts (budget ladder, case set, reference policies, control) cited to copies of evaluator code/contract in source_evidence/task/; implementation facts to source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py. No per-case, row-count or per-design baseline numbers used. Search score 0.914 kept in appendix as separate scope.\n- Rewrote claim: random/constant aggregate-only inference hedged; dropped speculation about tie rates; derived flip-vs-drop identity (math, labelled not empirical).\n- References: removed 4 off-topic entries, added 7 starter-literature entries (Bradley1952, Chiang2024, Zheng2023, Koh2017, Broderick2020, Min2025, Singh2025) with provenance ids ref.starter.*; deliberately did not cite or seek the excluded paper. 28 refs, all cited.\n- Figures: design_scores (matplotlib, PDF+PNG, scripts/build_figures.py); overview_protocol is an orchestrator-owned placeholder (guarded by .concept_figure_complete).\n- Validation: validate_claims passed (0 dangling evidence refs); validate_tex_refs passed; local pdflatex build: 8 body pages (last page ~full), refs p9-10, appendix p11-12. pdftotext missing locally so compile_latex.sh could not run; orchestrator must run the official compile.\n\n## Citation enrichment pass (2026-10-28)\n- Post-draft retrieval (Semantic Scholar rate-limited, OpenAlex fallback) added no refs to references.bib; I merged 5 OpenAlex hits whose abstracts I read: Yang2026Preference (label-flip preference poisoning), Seetharaman2021Influence (data poisoning definition), Giordano2018Swiss (infinitesimal jackknife finite-sample error bounds), Szczecinski2020GElo and Holy2026ScoreDriven (Elo-style sports rating). Dropped 3 over-supported BT refs (Davidson1969, Causeur2005, Yan2011) to stay at the 30-ref cap.\n- Rewrote only Related Work (new poisoning paragraph, Elo sentence, IJ sentence), additivity caveat (i), and one sentence in Evaluation designs. No numbers changed. Rejected claim: any home-advantage citation (no retrieved source supports it); stated BT fit has no home term.\n- Validation: validate_claims (14 claims, 30 refs), validate_tex_refs, evidence verifier pass; pdflatex: 9 body pages, conclusion on p9.\n\n## Repair pass (audit + figure manifest)\n- Removed ungrounded design size / \"six models\" claims (Table 1, competitors bullet); reworded permuted-control claim to the single control run per case; limited Yang2026Preference/Seetharaman2021Influence descriptions to title-supported content; softened Giordano/Holy/Szczecinski descriptions.\n- Concept-figure render failed (rc=1): shipped documented matplotlib fallback (scripts/build_overview.py, PDF+PNG), manifest uses list-valued categories/series_per_panel and generator=matplotlib, attempted_generator=generate-concept-figure.\n- validate_claims passes (14 claims, 30 refs); manifest validates against schema; paper compiles (13 pages incl. refs/SI).\n\n## Repair pass (audit round 3)\n- Fixed 2 audit findings: (1) L-BFGS cross-check now described as a single check on <=600 rows of the smallest case, not per edited refit/unedited case (Method, SI A); (2) evaluator influence baseline described as using the same first-order formulas with equal family-averaged score (0.606, margin 0.0), dropping \"numerically identical/same computation\" claims and disclosing the non-negative-effect filter and fitter difference; row-level edit-set identity not shown.\n- validate_claims passes; evidence verifier rerun; paper recompiles (13 pages). Soft figure-font warnings left as-is.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0411cd48-2d4b-4a08-9f9e-90c1b48083f1","uuid":"ce3378d6-780d-4dbb-a02d-c6ab077aa641","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:55:18.563Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval needs, none requiring a rewrite of the paper story.\n\n## Overall assessment\nThe draft is densely cited on Bradley-Terry estimation (ties, order effects, existence of the MLE, penalised fits, asymptotics) and on sampling uncertainty. It also cites the single-point influence-function paper, the dropping-data robustness paper, the two Arena integrity papers, and the Arena and MT-Bench data sources. All quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are out of scope here. The gaps concern the paper's actual subject (small adversarial edits to comparison data), the caveats on first-order approximations, and the sports data.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Poisoning and manipulation literature (high, positioning) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** the \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n- **Why:** the paper asks how few dropped or reversed rows displ