 $0.606$ for the influence search, a difference of $0.032$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_winrate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_influence]]\n208:The valid proposal rate is $1.0$: every answer to every budget level passed the format checks. The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$: on the single row-shuffled, relabelled control run per case, the proposer's scores matched its scores on the original order (mean absolute gap $0.0$). This is one permutation per case and does not establish invariance to row order or competitor labels in gener\n228:\\subsection{Expert human versus GPT-4 judge on the same questions}\n229:Expert human and GPT-4 judgments are on the same MT-Bench questions and models, which makes this the cleanest contrast in the study. For expert human data, reversal displaces rank 1 at 2\\% while dropping does not; for GPT-4 judgments neither works, and the edit success score is lower ($0.216$ versus $0.374$). The cause is untested. Differences in the number of judgments, the fraction of ties (\n241:\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVP2UyXzxh42dsEYRzSn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JMYFZ4wUgA9XtpnGKQg6mG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; sed -n 228,230p paper.tex; sed -n 241p paper.tex; grep -n -i \"abstract\" paper.tex | head -3; sed -n 50,58p paper.tex | cut -c1-600"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1121,"cache_read_input_tokens":45619,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1121,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0411cd48-2d4b-4a08-9f9e-90c1b48083f1","uuid":"f6295c18-4c79-48c5-8a01-1f8403f0b373","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:55:23.017Z","request_id":"req_011CgVP2UAg78NhBMpnXVhpi"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JMYFZ4wUgA9XtpnGKQg6mG","type":"tool_result","content":"\\subsection{Expert human versus GPT-4 judge on the same questions}\nExpert human and GPT-4 judgments are on the same MT-Bench questions and models, which makes this the cleanest contrast in the study. For expert human data, reversal displaces rank 1 at 2\\% while dropping does not; for GPT-4 judgments neither works, and the edit success score is lower ($0.216$ versus $0.374$). The cause is untested. Differences in the number of judgments, the fraction of ties (including the second tie label in the GPT-4 data), and the size of the rank-1 gap are all plausible, and each design has one case per mode, so this is a difference between two single measurements and not a statistical result about human versus LLM judges.\n\n\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source and the same questions.\n37:\\begin{abstract}\n45:\\end{abstract}\nTwo lines of concern motivate this paper.\nFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\nSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\nBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n\nWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\nThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\nA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\nThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0411cd48-2d4b-4a08-9f9e-90c1b48083f1","uuid":"8662a5aa-b519-49b1-86b8-b20a083fac18","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:55:23.085Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\subsection{Expert human versus GPT-4 judge on the same questions}\nExpert human and GPT-4 judgments are on the same MT-Bench questions and models, which makes this the cleanest contrast in the study. For expert human data, reversal displaces rank 1 at 2\\% while dropping does not; for GPT-4 judgments neither works, and the edit success score is lower ($0.216$ versus $0.374$). The cause is untested. Differences in the number of judgments, the fraction of ties (including the second tie label in the GPT-4 data), and the size of the rank-1 gap are all plausible, and each design has one case per mode, so this is a difference between two single measurements and not a statistical result about human versus LLM judges.\n\n\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source and the same questions.\n37:\\begin{abstract}\n45:\\end{abstract}\nTwo lines of concern motivate this paper.\nFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\nSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\nBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n\nWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\nThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\nA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\nThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
